  "preview": "Five lab solvents, freezing points listed in mixed units (°F and °C). Apply F = 1.8C + 32 to compare them on a single scale.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Apply a linear formula (F = 1.8C + 32) across signed numbers and unit conversions, then use the converted values to compare and reason about ordering.",
    "studentPrompt": "A chemistry teacher has five solvents in the lab. Their freezing points are listed in the table — but in mixed units. The relationship between Fahrenheit and Celsius is:\n\n  F = 1.8C + 32\n\n| Solvent | Freezing Point |\n|---|---|\n| Acetone | -94.3 °C |\n| Glycerol | 64.4 °F |\n| Carbon Disulfide | -168.7 °F |\n| Mercury | -38.83 °C |\n| Water | 32 °F |\n\n1. Convert every solvent's freezing point to °Celsius. Round to the nearest tenth.\n2. Order the solvents from lowest freezing point to highest.\n3. The lab is at -10 °C overnight. Which solvents will freeze? Which won't?\n4. By how many degrees Celsius is acetone's freezing point colder than mercury's?",
    "image": {
      "alt": "A simple thermometer with unlabeled tick marks beside a few falling snowflakes, suggesting a cold lab environment.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"#f8fafc\"/><g stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><rect x=\"150\" y=\"25\" width=\"24\" height=\"140\" rx=\"12\"/><circle cx=\"162\" cy=\"180\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#7dd3fc\" stroke=\"#0f172a\"/><line x1=\"162\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"162\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"#7dd3fc\" stroke-width=\"10\"/></g><g stroke=\"#475569\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><line x1=\"178\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"178\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"184\" y2=\"65\"/><line x1=\"178\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"85\"/><line x1=\"178\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"184\" y2=\"105\"/><line x1=\"178\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"125\"/><line x1=\"178\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"184\" y2=\"145\"/><line x1=\"178\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"160\"/></g><g stroke=\"#0ea5e9\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.85\"><g transform=\"translate(280,55)\"><line x1=\"-10\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"10\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-10\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"10\"/><line x1=\"-7\" y1=\"-7\" x2=\"7\" y2=\"7\"/><line x1=\"-7\" y1=\"7\" x2=\"7\" y2=\"-7\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(360,95)\"><line x1=\"-8\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"8\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-8\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"8\"/><line x1=\"-6\" y1=\"-6\" x2=\"6\" y2=\"6\"/><line x1=\"-6\" y1=\"6\" x2=\"6\" y2=\"-6\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(320,140)\"><line x1=\"-9\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"9\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-9\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"9\"/><line x1=\"-6\" y1=\"-6\" x2=\"6\" y2=\"6\"/><line x1=\"-6\" y1=\"6\" x2=\"6\" y2=\"-6\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(410,160)\"><line x1=\"-7\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"7\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-7\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"7\"/><line x1=\"-5\" y1=\"-5\" x2=\"5\" y2=\"5\"/><line x1=\"-5\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"5\" y2=\"-5\"/></g><g transform=\"translate(240,135)\"><line x1=\"-7\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"7\" y2=\"0\"/><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"-7\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"7\"/><line x1=\"-5\" y1=\"-5\" x2=\"5\" y2=\"5\"/><line x1=\"-5\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"5\" y2=\"-5\"/></g></g><g stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M40 200 Q 60 190 80 200 T 120 200\"/><path d=\"M40 210 Q 60 200 80 210 T 120 210\"/></g><g stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.9\"><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"75\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"85\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"65\" y2=\"85\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"95\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"90\" y2=\"105\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"105\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"106\" y2=\"68\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"114\" y2=\"68\"/></g></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show the formula F = 1.8C + 32. Don't pre-derive the inverse — let groups solve for C. Hand them the table.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Calculator allowed"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "formulas",
      "linear-equations",
      "signed-numbers",
      "unit-conversion"
    ],
    "taskType": "performance",
    "btcFit": "Performance task. Multi-step, requires algebra (rearranging the formula) plus signed-number arithmetic. Q3 surfaces a comparison move that goes beyond plug-and-chug.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Most groups will rearrange F = 1.8C + 32 into C = (F - 32) / 1.8 before tackling the °F entries.\n- Some groups will plug each F into the original formula and 'solve' separately each time — slower but valid.\n- Q3 requires comparing each Celsius value to -10 °C. Watch for sign errors.\n- Q4 requires |(-94.3) - (-38.83)| = 55.47 °C.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Forgetting to subtract 32 BEFORE dividing by 1.8 (gives wrong answer for F → C conversion).\n- Treating -168.7 °F as 'colder' than -94.3 °C without converting — they're in different units, you can't compare directly.\n- For Q4, subtracting raw values (-94.3 - 38.83 = -133.13) instead of taking absolute difference.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Why did we have to convert before comparing? What goes wrong if we don't?\n- Two groups rearranged the formula in different orders. Did they get the same answer? Why?\n- In Q3, the lab is at -10 °C. Which solvents are 'safe' from freezing? What real-world implication does this have for chemistry storage?\n- For Q4, why is the answer positive even though one of the temperatures is more negative?",
    "extensions": "- Suppose a chemist needs a solvent that stays liquid at -50 °C. Which solvents qualify?\n- A new solvent has a freezing point of -k °C. Express its Fahrenheit freezing point. For what k is the °F equivalent also negative?\n- What is the *one temperature* where Celsius and Fahrenheit read the same numerical value? (Solve C = 1.8C + 32. Answer: -40°.)",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Conversions:\n  - Acetone: -94.3 °C (already °C)\n  - Glycerol: (64.4 - 32) / 1.8 = 32.4 / 1.8 = 18 °C\n  - Carbon Disulfide: (-168.7 - 32) / 1.8 = -200.7 / 1.8 = -111.5 °C\n  - Mercury: -38.83 °C\n  - Water: (32 - 32) / 1.8 = 0 °C\n\n2. Order (low → high): Carbon Disulfide (-111.5), Acetone (-94.3), Mercury (-38.83), Water (0), Glycerol (18).\n3. At -10 °C, anything with freezing point ≥ -10 °C is at risk of freezing: Mercury (-38.83 °C) — no, still liquid; Water (0 °C) — yes, frozen; Glycerol (18 °C) — yes, frozen. Carbon Disulfide and Acetone stay liquid.\n4. |(-94.3) - (-38.83)| = |-55.47| = 55.47 °C colder.",
